Warmth was probably the first job. Small, active animals lose heat quickly, and fuzz holds a layer of warm air close to the skin. Later, colourful feathers became useful for display, to attract mates or warn rivals, like a [[@peacock-tail|peacock's tail]].

Feathers also kept eggs warm. Fossils of Oviraptor relatives show them crouched over nests, with their feathered arms spread around the eggs. And fast runners may have used feathered arms to help them balance and turn.

{{story:peacock-tail}}

On 24 December 1968, the crew of Apollo 8 became the first people to see Earth rise over the Moon's horizon. Astronaut Bill Anders grabbed a camera and took the photo now called Earthrise. It showed our planet small, blue and alone in black space. Many people say Earthrise helped inspire the environmental movement and [[@earth-day|the first Earth Day]] in 1970.

Four years later, on 7 December 1972, the Apollo 17 crew photographed the whole Earth lit by the Sun, with Africa and Antarctica clearly visible. This image, The Blue Marble, became one of the most shared photos in history.

{{story:voyager}}

Diamonds may form much closer to home. Deep inside Neptune and Uranus, the pressure is millions of times greater than on Earth's surface. Their atmospheres contain methane, a gas made of carbon and hydrogen.

Scientists think the pressure there may strip the carbon out and squeeze it into tiny diamonds, which sink like hailstones towards the planet's core. In 2017, researchers at SLAC in California blasted plastic with powerful lasers to recreate those conditions, and saw nanodiamonds form for a split second. Nobody can visit [[@neptune-winds|Neptune]] to check, so this is still an educated guess. If it's right, the diamonds may melt deeper down and form a layer of liquid carbon.

{{story:neptune-winds}}

In 1915, [[@einstein|Einstein]] showed that heavy objects bend space, and light travels along those bends. So a planet passing in front of a distant star acts like a magnifying glass. The star's light bends around the planet and looks brighter for a while.

For a big planet the brightening lasts a day or two. For one the size of Earth, it can be just a few hours. Telescopes have to stare at millions of stars at once to catch these flickers. Surveys from New Zealand, Chile and elsewhere have found dozens of possible rogue planets so far.

{{story:exoplanets}}

Migrating birds use several clues to [[@pigeons-homing|find their way]]. They watch the Sun by day and the stars by night. They remember coastlines, rivers and mountains. Many can also sense [[@earth-magnet|Earth's magnetism]], though exactly how is still being worked out. Young birds of many [[species|species]] make their first trip without a guide, so some of the route must be built in. Some birds may even use [[@nose-smell|smell]]. Experiments with seabirds called shearwaters suggest they find their way across open ocean partly by sniffing.

In 2013, scientists from the Wildlife Institute of India fitted three Amur falcons in Nagaland with satellite tags. The signals showed the birds crossing India and the Arabian Sea on their way to southern Africa.

{{story:earth-magnet}}

1927 में राल्फ़ पीयर नाम के एक रिकॉर्ड निर्माता ने ब्रिस्टल में एक ख़ाली इमारत में [[@behind-music-recording|रिकॉर्डिंग स्टूडियो]] लगाया। ब्रिस्टल टेनेसी और वर्जीनिया की सीमा पर बसा एक कस्बा है। उन्होंने स्थानीय अख़बार में विज्ञापन देकर संगीतकारों को रिकॉर्ड करने के लिए बुलाया।

जो लोग आए उनमें कार्टर फ़ैमिली थी, जो पहाड़ी गीत कसी हुई हार्मनी में गाती थी, और जिमी रॉजर्स थे, रेलवे के एक पुराने कर्मचारी जो योडल करते थे, यानी गले से आवाज़ ऊँची-नीची लहराते थे। दोनों सितारे बन गए। इतिहासकार ब्रिस्टल सेशन्स को अक्सर "कंट्री म्यूज़िक का बिग बैंग" कहते हैं, और आज ब्रिस्टल में इन पर एक संग्रहालय है।

{{story:appalachians}}

Instead of disappearing, painting changed. Since a camera could record exactly how things looked, painters began exploring what cameras couldn't do: colour, light, movement and feeling. The [[@impressionism|Impressionists]] borrowed from photography too, painting scenes cut off at odd angles, like snapshots.

Meanwhile, some photographers tried to make their pictures look like paintings, with soft focus and hand-worked prints, to prove photography was an art. Later photographers decided that photos should look like photos, sharp and clear.

{{story:cameras-how}}

मुख्य वास्तुकार आम तौर पर उस्ताद अहमद लाहौरी को माना जाता है, हालाँकि नक्शे पर कई जानकारों ने काम किया। इस इमारत में फ़ारसी, मध्य एशियाई और भारतीय विचार घुले-मिले हैं: मेहराबदार ऊँचा दरवाज़ा जिसे *ईवान* कहते हैं, प्याज़ जैसा फूला हुआ गुंबद, और भारतीय वास्तुकला से आई, गुंबद वाली छोटी-छोटी *छतरियाँ*।

लिखावट भी डिज़ाइन का हिस्सा थी। बड़े मेहराबों के चारों ओर काले पत्थर से जड़ी क़ुरान की आयतें हैं। [[calligraphy|सुलेख]] करने वाले अमानत ख़ाँ ने ऊपर के अक्षर नीचे वालों से बड़े बनाए, ताकि ज़मीन से देखने पर सब एक ही आकार के दिखें। यह पत्थर में उकेरी गई [[@perspective|परिप्रेक्ष्य]] की एक तरकीब है।

{{story:air-pollution}}

In cities, many vets work in pet clinics, looking after dogs, cats, birds and fish. On farms, vets travel to treat cattle, buffaloes, sheep and poultry. India had about 53 crore farm animals in its 2019 livestock [[census|census]], and many families depend on them for milk and income. Government vets run village clinics and vaccination drives to keep these herds healthy.

Other vets work with wildlife. In tiger reserves like Ranthambore or Kaziranga, wildlife vets treat injured animals and help move them safely. Some vets work in labs, studying diseases or checking that meat and milk are safe to eat.

{{story:dogs-domestic}}

If you double one number and halve the other, a multiplication answer stays the same. So 25 × 16 becomes 50 × 8, then 100 × 4, which is 400. To multiply by 5, multiply by 10 and halve. To find 15% of something, find 10%, then add half of that again.

These moves feel like magic at first. They work because of how multiplication is built, and they show why understanding numbers beats memorising steps.

{{story:memory}}

Anders Celsius was a Swedish [[astronomer|astronomer]]. In 1742 he suggested a simpler scale with 100 steps between the freezing and boiling points of water. But in his version, water boiled at 0 and froze at 100. Colder things had higher numbers.

Celsius died in 1744. Soon after, other scientists, including the famous Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, turned the scale the right way up. For a long time it was called "centigrade", meaning "a hundred steps". In 1948, it was officially renamed Celsius in his honour.

{{story:thermometer}}

Venice, in Italy, held the first film festival in 1932, on the Lido, a long sandy island next to [[@venice|Venice]]. It still runs every late summer. Cannes, on the French Riviera, was meant to start in 1939 but was cancelled when the Second World War began. It finally opened in 1946. Its top prize, the Palme d'Or, or Golden Palm, is one of the most famous awards in cinema.

Berlin started in 1951 and gives out Golden Bears. Toronto, in Canada, is known for sharing the public's favourite films, and many go on to win [[@oscars|Oscars]]. Sundance, in the United States, is famous for independent films made outside the big studios.

{{story:venice}}

Each team gets a turn to chase and a turn to defend, usually twice. The defending team sends in three players at a time. When all three are out, the next three come in.

Defenders dodge, weave between the seated chasers and run round the poles. They try to stay "alive" as long as possible. The chasing team scores a point for every defender tagged, and the team with the most points at the end wins. Under newer rules, a defender who survives long enough can also earn points.

{{story:reflexes}}

Try balancing a top on its point without spinning it. It falls over at once. Spin it fast, and it stands on that tiny point for a minute or more.

Each bit of the top is moving in a circle. When gravity pulls the top sideways, those moving bits carry the push around with them. So instead of tipping, the top wobbles in a slow circle. Scientists call that wobble precession. A moving [[@bicycle-balance|bicycle]] gets some help from its spinning wheels in a similar way, though steering does most of the work.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

अलार्म बजते ही [[@nervous-system|तंत्रिका तंत्र]] और [[gland|ग्रंथियाँ]] काम पर लग जाती हैं। गुर्दों के ऊपर बैठी ग्रंथियाँ [[adrenaline|एड्रेनलिन]] छोड़ती हैं, एक [[hormone|हॉर्मोन]] जो दिल और साँस की रफ़्तार बढ़ा देता है। जिगर खून में शक्कर छोड़ता है, ताकि अतिरिक्त ऊर्जा मिले। पुतलियाँ फैल जाती हैं, ताकि ज़्यादा रोशनी अंदर आए।

खून पेट से हटाकर हाथ-पैरों की मांसपेशियों की ओर भेजा जाता है। इसीलिए डर में पेट में गुदगुदी या गाँठ-सी महसूस होती है। तुम्हें पसीना आ सकता है, तुम काँप सकते हो या तुम्हारे [[@goosebumps|रोंगटे खड़े हो सकते हैं]]। 1915 में अमेरिकी वैज्ञानिक वॉल्टर कैनन ने इसे "लड़ो या भागो" प्रतिक्रिया कहा। अब वैज्ञानिक एक तीसरा रास्ता भी जोड़ते हैं: जम जाओ, यानी एकदम स्थिर रहो और उम्मीद करो कि किसी की नज़र न पड़े।

{{story:courage}}

हूबहू [[@twins|जुड़वाँ]] के सारे जीन एक जैसे होते हैं, इसलिए हाथ की पसंद समझने में वे बहुत काम आते हैं। अगर बात सिर्फ़ जीन की होती, तो दोनों हमेशा एक जैसे होते। पर ऐसा नहीं है। लगभग हर पाँच में से एक जोड़ी में एक जुड़वाँ खब्बू होता है और दूसरा दाएँ हाथ वाला।

इससे वैज्ञानिक समझते हैं कि पूरी कहानी [[@genes-family|जीन]] की नहीं है। शिशु के विकास में होने वाले छोटे-छोटे, बेतरतीब फ़र्क भी भूमिका निभाते लगते हैं। खब्बू होने का ज़्यादा या कम होशियार होने से कोई रिश्ता नहीं। और हाथ की पसंद पूरी तरह इधर या उधर भी नहीं होती। बहुत-से लोग एक हाथ से लिखते हैं, पर दूसरे से बल्लेबाज़ी करते, किक मारते या फेंकते हैं, और कुछ लोग दोनों हाथों से बराबर आराम से काम कर लेते हैं।

{{story:cricket-bat}}

[[@body-no-water|Losing too much water]] is called dehydration. Mild dehydration can bring a headache, tiredness and trouble concentrating. It happens more easily in hot weather, when you're running around, or when you're ill with a [[@fever|fever]], vomiting or diarrhoea.

Babies and young children dehydrate faster than adults because they are small and lose water quickly. One of the great medical discoveries of the 1900s was oral rehydration solution, a simple mix of clean water, [[@salt|salt]] and sugar. Research in Kolkata and Dhaka helped show it worked, and it has since saved millions of children's lives around the world.

{{story:water-cycle}}

On a rocky hill above the valley sit older stone walls that weave between huge natural boulders. This was probably where the kings lived and where important ceremonies took place. From the top you can see across the whole valley.

Ordinary people lived in round houses of clay and thatch spread around the stone walls. The stone was mostly for the rich and powerful.

{{story:ancient-africa}}

Tulips flower in spring, and bulbs can only be dug up safely in summer. So deals made in autumn and winter were promises: I will pay you this much for that bulb when it comes out of the ground. Traders met in taverns, where contracts changed hands over drinks.

People called it windhandel, the "wind trade", because nothing real changed hands. Prices for some bulbs rose many times over in a few months. A single Semper Augustus bulb was reportedly priced at thousands of guilders, about what a skilled worker earned in many years.

{{story:stock-market}}

Edinburgh Castle sits high on Castle Rock, the plug of an ancient [[@volcano|volcano]] that erupted about 350 million years ago. Across the city rises Arthur's Seat, another old volcano, where people walk up for the view. Neither has erupted for hundreds of millions of years.

Every August the city hosts the Edinburgh Festival Fringe, one of the largest arts festivals in the world, with thousands of shows in theatres, pubs and even on buses. J.K. Rowling wrote parts of the first Harry Potter books in Edinburgh cafés.

{{story:television-invented}}

Around 200 BCE, Aristophanes of Byzantium, who ran the great library in Alexandria, suggested a system of dots for reading aloud. A dot high up on the line meant a long pause. A dot in the middle meant a short pause, and a low dot a medium one. You might call it the great-grandparent of the full stop, colon and comma.

Even our names for marks come from Greek. *Komma* meant "a piece cut off", *kolon* meant "a limb" and *periodos* meant "going round". The library itself was one of the [[@great-libraries|great libraries]] of the ancient world.

{{story:great-libraries}}

Oxford scientist Charles Spence studies what he calls gastrophysics, the science of how our senses shape what we eat. His experiments show that crisps taste fresher when the crunch is louder, and that the weight and colour of a plate change how food seems to taste.

Touch is part of this too. Your fingertips are packed with [[sensor|sensors]], among the most sensitive parts of your body. They tell you about temperature, texture and wetness. Some researchers think that touching food before eating it makes us pay more attention, which can make the first bite more satisfying. The research is still young, and results differ from person to person.

{{story:taste-smell-link}}

अमेरिका के शुरुआती सिनेमा हॉलों के कई मालिक पॉपकॉर्न नहीं चाहते थे, क्योंकि उससे गंदगी फैलती थी। पर 1930 के दशक की [[@great-depression|महामंदी]] के दौरान पॉपकॉर्न उन गिनी-चुनी चीज़ों में से था जिसे ज़्यादातर लोग ख़रीद सकते थे, और ठेले वाले सिनेमा हॉलों के बाहर खड़े होने लगे। जल्दी ही सिनेमा हॉल खुद पॉपकॉर्न बेचने लगे, और इसी से कई हॉल बंद होने से बच गए।

{{story:microwave}}

1940 के दशक में अमेरिकी इंजीनियर पर्सी स्पेंसर माइक्रोवेव बनाने वाली मशीनों की जाँच कर रहे थे। उन्होंने उनसे मक्का फोड़कर देखा, और यह काम कर गया। 1980 के दशक में माइक्रोवेव पॉपकॉर्न के पैकेट लोकप्रिय हुए, और आज [[@hollywood|हॉलीवुड]] से लेकर हैदराबाद तक हर सिनेमा हॉल में पॉपकॉर्न बिकता है।

By the 1600s, cooks had discovered a shortcut: add vinegar to fresh rice and you get the sour taste at once, with no waiting. Then, in the early 1800s, street stalls in Edo, the city now called [[@tokyo|Tokyo]], began selling nigiri sushi. A cook pressed a little rice into a block by hand and laid a slice of fresh fish from Tokyo Bay on top.

It was quick, cheap food for busy workers, eaten standing up. A chef named Hanaya Yohei is often given credit for popularising it. Today the same style can [[@food-expensive|cost a fortune]] in top restaurants.

{{story:tokyo}}
